"CanaaniteDeitiesandReligion","title":"Canaanite Deities and Religion"},{"id":"Dagon","title":"Dagon"},{"id":"EkronEkronites","title":"Ekron, Ekronites*"},{"id":"Emerod","title":"Emerod*"},{"id":"Gath","title":"Gath"},{"id":"Monotheism","title":"Monotheism*"},{"id":"PhilistiaPhilistines","title":"Philistia, Philistines"},{"id":"Plague","title":"Plague"},{"id":"SamuelPerson","title":"Samuel (Person)"},{"id":"SamuelBooksofFirstandSecond","title":"Samuel, Books of First and Second"},{"id":"Tumor","title":"Tumor"}],"dictionaryWords":[{"evidence":[{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"Ebenezer","senseEvidence":{"basis":"source_verse_citation","originalCitations":[{"anchorIndex":2,"href":"?bref=1Sam.5.1","rawReference":"1Sam.5.1"}],"ruleId":"Ebenezer::Ebenezer::source_verse_citation","sourceHeadwordId":"Ebenezer"},"text":"After the Philistines had captured the ark of God, they took it from Ebenezer to Ashdod,","verse":1}],"id":"Ebenezer","label":"Ebenezer","matchReviews":[{"ruleId":"Ebenezer::Ebenezer::source_verse_citation","scope":"source_verse_citation","senseReview":"Original complete headword and source opening identify a named person, people, or place. Promotion requires a capitalized whole name and the original article explicitly citing this same BSB verse. No arbitrary qualifier stripping or book-title inference. This citation-constrained rule was reviewed once globally; unmatched contexts remain excluded.","sourceHeadwordId":"Ebenezer","term":"Ebenezer"}],"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","matchingRules":["reviewed_exact_headword"],"sourceProvenanceId":"Ebenezer","sourceTitle":"Ebenezer"},{"evidence":[{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"LORD","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Lord::LORD::reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","sourceHeadwordId":"Lord"},"text":"When the people of Ashdod got up early the next morning, there was Dagon, fallen on his face before the ark of the LORD. So they took Dagon and returned him to his place.","verse":3},{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"LORD","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Lord::LORD::reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","sourceHeadwordId":"Lord"},"text":"But when they got up early the next morning, there was Dagon, fallen on his face before the ark of the LORD, with his head and his hands broken off and lying on the threshold. Only the torso remained.","verse":4},{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"LORD","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Lord::LORD::reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","sourceHeadwordId":"Lord"},"text":"Now the hand of the LORD was heavy on the people of Ashdod and its vicinity, ravaging them and afflicting them with tumors.","verse":6},{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"LORD","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Lord::LORD::reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","sourceHeadwordId":"Lord"},"text":"But after they had moved the ark to Gath, the LORD’s hand was also against that city, throwing it into great confusion and afflicting the men of the city, both young and old, with an outbreak of tumors.","verse":9}],"id":"Lord","label":"LORD","matchReviews":[{"ruleId":"Lord::LORD::reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","scope":"reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","senseReview":"Only literal uppercase LORD is admitted globally. Source Lord article explicitly covers God as Lord; lowercase human lord and sentence-title Lord are not inferred from this rule.","sourceHeadwordId":"Lord","term":"LORD"}],"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","matchingRules":["reviewed_exact_headword"],"sourceProvenanceId":"Lord","sourceTitle":"Lord"}],"format":"bsb-study-chapter-v2","notes":[{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":["The Ark of the Covenant"],"sourceClass":"theme-title","t":"h"},{"c":["The Ark of the Covenant was a wooden chest overlaid with gold, and it was placed within the Most Holy Place in the sanctuary. The Ark reminded Israel of God’s covenant with them; its contents included the tablets of the covenant (the Ten Commandments). On the Day of Atonement, the high priest would sprinkle blood on the Ark’s cover in order to purify the people (",{"href":"?bref=Lev.16.2-17","r":"Lev.16.2-17","rawR":"Lev.16.2-17","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Lev.16.2-17","value":"Lev.16.2-17"}],"t":"ref","v":"Lev 16:2-17"},")."],"sourceClass":"theme-body-fl","t":"p"},{"c":["During Israel’s wilderness wanderings, the glory of God would appear over the Ark, and God would meet with his people there (see ",{"href":"?bref=Exod.25.22","r":"Exod.25.22","rawR":"Exod.25.22","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Exod.25.22","value":"Exod.25.22"}],"t":"ref","v":"Exod 25:22"},"). Throughout Israel’s history, the Ark served as a sign of God’s presence. It was carried before the people as they traveled (",{"href":"?bref=Num.10.33-36","r":"Num.10.33-36","rawR":"Num.10.33-36","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Num.10.33-36","value":"Num.10.33-36"}],"t":"ref","v":"Num 10:33-36"},"), and when it was brought to the Jordan River, the water stopped up so the Israelites could pass through on dry ground (",{"href":"?bref=Josh.3.4-6","r":"Josh.3.4-6","rawR":"Josh.3.4-6","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Josh.3.4-6","value":"Josh.3.4-6"}],"t":"ref","v":"Josh 3:4-6"},"). The Ark was carried around Jericho before that city was invaded and was present during the siege of the Ammonite city of Rabbah during David’s time (",{"href":"?bref=2Sam.11.1-11","r":"2Sam.11.1-11","rawR":"2Sam.11.1-11","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Sam.11.1-11","value":"2Sam.11.1-11"}],"t":"ref","v":"2 Sam 11:1-11"},")."],"sourceClass":"theme-body","t":"p"},{"c":["The Philistines equated the Ark with Israel’s God (",{"href":"?bref=1Sam.4.6-8","r":"1Sam.4.6-8","rawR":"1Sam.4.6-8","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Sam.4.6-8","value":"1Sam.4.6-8"}],"t":"ref","v":"1 Sam 4:6-8"},"). This is understandable because the Philistines brought images of their own gods into battle (",{"href":"?bref=2Sam.5.21","r":"2Sam.5.21","rawR":"2Sam.5.21","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Sam.5.21","value":"2Sam.5.21"}],"t":"ref","v":"2 Sam 5:21"},"). The Israelites were not to use images of the divine, so they viewed the Ark as the Lord’s footstool and temporary residence rather than his image (see ",{"href":"?bref=1Chr.28.2","r":"1Chr.28.2","rawR":"1Chr.28.2","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Chr.28.2","value":"1Chr.28.2"}],"t":"ref","v":"1 Chr 28:2"},"). However, the Israelites developed an idolatrous, pagan point of view. They treated the sacred symbol as a fetish and tried to manipulate God’s presence for their own benefit. They reasoned, “If the Ark is with us, then God’s presence and favor in battle will also be with us” (see ",{"href":"?bref=1Sam.4.3","r":"1Sam.4.3","rawR":"1Sam.4.3","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Sam.4.3","value":"1Sam.4.3"}],"t":"ref","v":"1 Sam 4:3"},"). As their defeat that day showed (",{"href":"?bref=1Sam.4.1-11","r":"1Sam.4.1-11","rawR":"1Sam.4.1-11","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Sam.4.1-11","value":"1Sam.4.1-11"}],"t":"ref","v":"4:1-11"},"), God cannot be manipulated—he is the ruler of his people, and he must be obeyed and trusted (cp. ",{"href":"?bref=Josh.5.13-15","r":"Josh.5.13-15","rawR":"Josh.5.13-15","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Josh.5.13-15","value":"Josh.5.13-15"}],"t":"ref","v":"Josh 5:13-15"},")."],"sourceClass":"theme-body","t":"p"},{"c":["Eventually, King David moved the Ark to Jerusalem (",{"href":"?bref=2Sam.6.1-17","r":"2Sam.6.1-17","rawR":"2Sam.6.1-17","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Sam.6.1-17","value":"2Sam.6.1-17"}],"t":"ref","v":"2 Sam 6:1-17"},"). When Solomon built the Temple, the Ark was placed in the Most Holy Place, and it continued to play a central role in Israelite worship (",{"href":"?bref=1Kgs.8.1-11","r":"1Kgs.8.1-11","rawR":"1Kgs.8.1-11","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Kgs.8.1-11","value":"1Kgs.8.1-11"}],"t":"ref","v":"1 Kgs 8:1-11"},"). After the early monarchical period, the Ark is not mentioned again. Presumably, it was destroyed by the Babylonians along with the Temple in 586 ",{"c":["BC"],"sourceClass":"era","t":"span"},", though an apocryphal tradition claimed that the prophet Jeremiah had hidden the Ark at the time of the Exile (",{"href":"?bref=2Macc.2.4-8","r":"2Macc.2.4-8","rawR":"2Macc.2.4-8","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Macc.2.4-8","value":"2Macc.2.4-8"}],"t":"ref","v":"2 Macc 2:4-8"},"). Regardless, the arrival of God’s presence through Jesus Christ and the all-sufficient nature of his sacrifice rendered the Ark unnecessary (see ",{"href":"?bref=Heb.9.1-10.18","r":"Heb.9.1-10.18","rawR":"Heb.9.1-10.18","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Heb.9.1-10.18","value":"Heb.9.1-10.18"}],"t":"ref","v":"Heb 9:1–10:18"},")."],"sourceClass":"theme-body","t":"p"},{"c":["Passages for Further Study"],"sourceClass":"theme-refs-title","t":"h"},{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Exod.25.10-22","r":"Exod.25.10-22","rawR":"Exod.25.10-22","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Exod.25.10-22","value":"Exod.25.10-22"}],"t":"ref","v":"Exod 25:10-22"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Exod.40.20-21","r":"Exod.40.20-21","rawR":"Exod.40.20-21","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Exod.40.20-21","value":"Exod.40.20-21"}],"t":"ref","v":"40:20-21"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Lev.16.2","r":"Lev.16.2","rawR":"Lev.16.2","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Lev.16.2","value":"Lev.16.2"}],"t":"ref","v":"Lev 16:2"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Num.10.33-36","r":"Num.10.33-36","rawR":"Num.10.33-36","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Num.10.33-36","value":"Num.10.33-36"}],"t":"ref","v":"Num 10:33-36"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Deut.31.25-26","r":"Deut.31.25-26","rawR":"Deut.31.25-26","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Deut.31.25-26","value":"Deut.31.25-26"}],"t":"ref","v":"Deut 31:25-26"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Josh.3.4-6","r":"Josh.3.4-6","rawR":"Josh.3.4-6","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Josh.3.4-6","value":"Josh.3.4-6"}],"t":"ref","v":"Josh 3:4-6"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Josh.6.6-13","r":"Josh.6.6-13","rawR":"Josh.6.6-13","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Josh.6.6-13","value":"Josh.6.6-13"}],"t":"ref","v":"6:6-13"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Judg.20.26-28","r":"Judg.20.26-28","rawR":"Judg.20.26-28","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Judg.20.26-28","value":"Judg.20.26-28"}],"t":"ref","v":"Judg 20:26-28"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=1Sam.4.3-11","r":"1Sam.4.3-11","rawR":"1Sam.4.3-11","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Sam.4.3-11","value":"1Sam.4.3-11"}],"t":"ref","v":"1 Sam 4:3-11"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=1Sam.5.1-7.2","r":"1Sam.5.1-7.2","rawR":"1Sam.5.1-7.2","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Sam.5.1-7.2","value":"1Sam.5.1-7.2"}],"t":"ref","v":"5:1–7:2"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=2Sam.6.1-17","r":"2Sam.6.1-17","rawR":"2Sam.6.1-17","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Sam.6.1-17","value":"2Sam.6.1-17"}],"t":"ref","v":"2 Sam 6:1-17"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Jer.3.16-18","r":"Jer.3.16-18","rawR":"Jer.3.16-18","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Jer.3.16-18","value":"Jer.3.16-18"}],"t":"ref","v":"Jer 3:16-18"}],"sourceClass":"theme-refs","t":"p"}],"id":"ThemeNote:TheArkOfTheCovenant","refs":[{"book":"1SA","endChapter":7,"endVerse":2,"startChapter":4,"startVerse":1}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"TheArkOfTheCovenant","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"ThemeNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"1SA","sourceChapterCoverage":[4,5,6,7],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"467dae4de5deed672a3b9039ed07b7a33996db0310f2048e65e27d82747892db","sourceItemName":"TheArkOfTheCovenant","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/ThemeNotes.xml","sourceOrder":57,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"1SA","canonicalOSIS":"1Sam","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"1Sam"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"1Sam.4.1-7.2","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":"The Ark of the Covenant","supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"The Ark of the Covenant","type":"ThemeNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":["Canaanite Religion"],"sourceClass":"theme-title","t":"h"},{"c":["The Canaanites explained nature by reference to their gods. Each god represented some force of nature. The moon, sun, important stars, and visible planets each were considered a god or goddess. Canaanite deities had two striking features: an extraordinary fluidity of personality, and names whose meanings were related to their function. A few of these gods were particularly influential in the interaction of the Israelites with the Canaanite peoples:"],"sourceClass":"theme-body-fl","t":"p"},{"c":[{"c":["El:"],"sourceClass":"bold","t":"span"}," “the powerful one,” the head of the Canaanite pantheon. El was distant, living far away from Canaan “at the source of the two rivers” (a lingering memory of Eden, perhaps). He presided over a divine council of gods who were his children. In the Old Testament, ",{"c":["El"],"sourceClass":"ital","t":"span"}," (pl. ",{"c":["Elohim"],"sourceClass":"ital","t":"span"},") is used to speak of God, though the Lord is not identical with the Canaanite deity."],"sourceClass":"theme-list-sp","t":"p"},{"c":[{"c":["Baal:"],"sourceClass":"bold","t":"span"}," “lord,” the great storm god and king of the gods. Baal acted as El’s prime minister and was functionally far more important than El. He was the giver of all fertility. When he died, all vegetation and procreation temporarily ceased. He was the god of justice, the terror of evildoers."],"sourceClass":"theme-list","t":"p"},{"c":[{"c":["Dagon:"],"sourceClass":"bold","t":"span"}," “fish” or “grain,” sometimes referred to as the father of Baal. Dagon was the chief god of the Philistine city of Ashdod (",{"href":"?bref=1Sam.5.1-7","r":"1Sam.5.1-7","rawR":"1Sam.5.1-7","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Sam.5.1-7","value":"1Sam.5.1-7"}],"t":"ref","v":"1 Sam 5:1-7"},")."],"sourceClass":"theme-list","t":"p"},{"c":[{"c":["Ashtoreth, Anath, Asherah:"],"sourceClass":"bold","t":"span"}," the three main goddesses of the Canaanite pantheon, concerned mainly with sex and war. Their primary function was to have sexual relations with Baal on a continual yearly cycle, maintaining Baal’s virility and thus the fertility of the earth."],"sourceClass":"theme-list","t":"p"},{"c":["The Canaanites’ personification of the forces of nature accounted for the succession of the seasons. The dry period from April to the end of October represented the duration of Baal’s death after an unsuccessful battle each spring. Commencement of the autumn rains signaled Baal’s revival as the rain and vegetation god, continuing until the following April. The Canaanites believed that the land regained its fertility because of the annual mating of Baal and his female consort, and their own religious activities included imitation of their gods’ behavior."],"sourceClass":"theme-body-sp","t":"p"},{"c":["The Canaanites thus worshiped things they felt would benefit them materially. If the gods and goddesses were pleased by the worship, the result would be a plentiful harvest. If a god became angry, animal or human sacrifice might appease the god (see ",{"href":"?bref=2Kgs.3.26-27","r":"2Kgs.3.26-27","rawR":"2Kgs.3.26-27","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Kgs.3.26-27","value":"2Kgs.3.26-27"}],"t":"ref","v":"2 Kgs 3:26-27"},"). Canaanite worship was therefore fundamentally based on fear and manipulation."],"sourceClass":"theme-body","t":"p"},{"c":["Contact among the many religions of the ancient Near East produced not only social tension but also religious borrowing. The Arameans and Philistines who settled in Canaan adopted the practices of the Canaanites; the Amorites similarly incorporated Sumerian religion when they moved into Mesopotamia."],"sourceClass":"theme-body","t":"p"},{"c":["Israel’s God, Yahweh, by contrast, demanded exclusive allegiance, and he insisted on worship based on faith rather than manipulative fear. Unfortunately, the Israelites failed to maintain their exclusive monotheism. They adopted pagan thinking and practices, worshiping the Canaanite gods in the Canaanite way, leading to the corruption of their culture and rousing God’s anger. As a result, God brought judgment upon his people, sending them into exile (see ",{"href":"?bref=2Kgs.17.5-23","r":"2Kgs.17.5-23","rawR":"2Kgs.17.5-23","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Kgs.17.5-23","value":"2Kgs.17.5-23"}],"t":"ref","v":"2 Kgs 17:5-23"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=2Kgs.24.18-25.21","r":"2Kgs.24.18-25.21","rawR":"2Kgs.24.18-25.21","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Kgs.24.18-25.21","value":"2Kgs.24.18-25.21"}],"t":"ref","v":"24:18–25:21"},")."],"sourceClass":"theme-body","t":"p"},{"c":["Passages for Further Study"],"sourceClass":"theme-refs-title","t":"h"},{"c":[{"href":"?bref=1Sam.5.1-12","r":"1Sam.5.1-12","rawR":"1Sam.5.1-12","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Sam.5.1-12","value":"1Sam.5.1-12"}],"t":"ref","v":"1 Sam 5:1-12"}],"sourceClass":"theme-refs","t":"p"}],"id":"ThemeNote:CanaaniteReligion","refs":[{"book":"1SA","endChapter":5,"endVerse":12,"startChapter":5,"startVerse":1}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"CanaaniteReligion","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"ThemeNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"1SA","sourceChapterCoverage":[5],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"0214b94d9255409cd75c10ec44d96eb21cdbfcbff408d4bad32c994a1db94a7a","sourceItemName":"CanaaniteReligion","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/ThemeNotes.xml","sourceOrder":63,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"1SA","canonicalOSIS":"1Sam","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"1Sam"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"1Sam.5.1-12","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":"Canaanite Religion","supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Canaanite Religion","type":"ThemeNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=1Sam.5.1","r":"1Sam.5.1","rawR":"1Sam.5.1","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Sam.5.1","value":"1Sam.5.1"}],"t":"ref","v":"5:1"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["Ebenezer:"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," See ",{"href":"?item=ISam.4.1_StudyNote_Filament","id":"ISam.4.1_StudyNote_Filament","rawId":"ISam.4.1_StudyNote_Filament","t":"link","v":"study note on 4:1b"},". • ",{"c":["Ashdod"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"},{"c":[","],"sourceClass":"ital","t":"span"}," one of the five important Philistine cities (see ",{"href":"?bref=1Sam.6.17","r":"1Sam.6.17","rawR":"1Sam.6.17","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Sam.6.17","value":"1Sam.6.17"}],"t":"ref","v":"6:17"},"), was located along the same major trade highway (the Great Trunk Road) as Aphek."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:ISam.5.1","refs":[{"book":"1SA","endChapter":5,"endVerse":1,"startChapter":5,"startVerse":1}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"ISam.5.1","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"1SA","sourceChapterCoverage":[5],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"5aa9dcf1788c968d3857d606f2fc4927c69d9b811d3cde60c97d8c2daec90c2d","sourceItemName":"ISam.5.1","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":3274,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"1SA","canonicalOSIS":"1Sam","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"1Sam"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"1Sam.5.1","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"ISam.5.1","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=1Sam.5.2","r":"1Sam.5.2","rawR":"1Sam.5.2","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Sam.5.2","value":"1Sam.5.2"}],"t":"ref","v":"5:2"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["Dagon"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," was one of the Philistines’ gods (see ",{"href":"?bref=Judg.16.23-24","r":"Judg.16.23-24","rawR":"Judg.16.23-24","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Judg.16.23-24","value":"Judg.16.23-24"}],"t":"ref","v":"Judg 16:23-24"},"). The name could be related to the Hebrew ",{"c":["dag"],"sourceClass":"hebrew","t":"span"}," (“fish”), an appropriate name for a god of seafaring people. More likely, it reflects the Hebrew ",{"c":["dagan"],"sourceClass":"hebrew","t":"span"}," (“grain”), suggesting a fertility or agriculture god."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:ISam.5.2","refs":[{"book":"1SA","endChapter":5,"endVerse":2,"startChapter":5,"startVerse":2}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"ISam.5.2","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"1SA","sourceChapterCoverage":[5],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"27217c0b1c24dec38c6d0afc4764bd7262877be0939758a0da2712fa70244cbc","sourceItemName":"ISam.5.2","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":3275,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"1SA","canonicalOSIS":"1Sam","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"1Sam"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"1Sam.5.2","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"ISam.5.2","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=1Sam.5.4","r":"1Sam.5.4","rawR":"1Sam.5.4","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Sam.5.4","value":"1Sam.5.4"}],"t":"ref","v":"5:4"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["his head and hands had broken off:"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," A symbol of God’s supremacy over the idol (",{"href":"?bref=1Sam.5.7","r":"1Sam.5.7","rawR":"1Sam.5.7","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Sam.5.7","value":"1Sam.5.7"}],"t":"ref","v":"5:7"},"; see also ",{"href":"?bref=1Sam.17.51","r":"1Sam.17.51","rawR":"1Sam.17.51","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Sam.17.51","value":"1Sam.17.51"}],"t":"ref","v":"17:51"},")."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:ISam.5.4","refs":[{"book":"1SA","endChapter":5,"endVerse":4,"startChapter":5,"startVerse":4}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"ISam.5.4","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"1SA","sourceChapterCoverage":[5],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"3962157393b71248e1954bb950de6f69ddadbf33db2fcde69e50252b35f2eeb5","sourceItemName":"ISam.5.4","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":3276,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"1SA","canonicalOSIS":"1Sam","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"1Sam"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"1Sam.5.4","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"ISam.5.4","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=1Sam.5.5","r":"1Sam.5.5","rawR":"1Sam.5.5","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Sam.5.5","value":"1Sam.5.5"}],"t":"ref","v":"5:5"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["step on its threshold:"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," While the Philistines might have believed that Dagon’s amputations resulted from their failure to honor the god properly, the true failure was in worshiping an idol rather than honoring the one true God (",{"href":"?bref=Isa.44.6-20","r":"Isa.44.6-20","rawR":"Isa.44.6-20","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Isa.44.6-20","value":"Isa.44.6-20"}],"t":"ref","v":"Isa 44:6-20"},")."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:ISam.5.5","refs":[{"book":"1SA","endChapter":5,"endVerse":5,"startChapter":5,"startVerse":5}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"ISam.5.5","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"1SA","sourceChapterCoverage":[5],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"5f2b04e4827714130cea6cc8249a717a7d0c57f1a425f604df78ebf369ae0f12","sourceItemName":"ISam.5.5","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":3277,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"1SA","canonicalOSIS":"1Sam","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"1Sam"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"1Sam.5.5","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"ISam.5.5","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=1Sam.5.6","r":"1Sam.5.6","rawR":"1Sam.5.6","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Sam.5.6","value":"1Sam.5.6"}],"t":"ref","v":"5:6"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["tumors:"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," The Hebrew term can mean simply “swellings.” A possible alternative translation is “hemorrhoids.” They were possibly the fatal ",{"c":["buboes"],"sourceClass":"ital","t":"span"}," of bubonic plague, which attack the lower body, especially the rectal area. Rats are known carriers of the plague."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:ISam.5.6","refs":[{"book":"1SA","endChapter":5,"endVerse":6,"startChapter":5,"startVerse":6}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"ISam.5.6","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"1SA","sourceChapterCoverage":[5],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"ec2fc7795198bfc604c922a37b1422b40079f6cd926f3f1da204dff8e3c19198","sourceItemName":"ISam.5.6","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":3278,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"1SA","canonicalOSIS":"1Sam","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"1Sam"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"1Sam.5.6","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"ISam.5.6","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=1Sam.5.8","r":"1Sam.5.8","rawR":"1Sam.5.8","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Sam.5.8","value":"1Sam.5.8"}],"t":"ref","v":"5:8"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["rulers"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," (or ",{"c":["overlords"],"sourceClass":"ital","t":"span"},"): Each of the five major Philistine cities had its own ruler (",{"href":"?bref=1Sam.6.16-18","r":"1Sam.6.16-18","rawR":"1Sam.6.16-18","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Sam.6.16-18","value":"1Sam.6.16-18"}],"t":"ref","v":"6:16-18"},"). • ",{"c":["Move it:"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," Despite the obvious danger, the Philistines relished their captured trophy enough to relocate it rather than return it. • ",{"c":["Gath"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"},{"c":[","],"sourceClass":"ital","t":"span"}," one of the five major Philistine cities, was nearby."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:ISam.5.8","refs":[{"book":"1SA","endChapter":5,"endVerse":8,"startChapter":5,"startVerse":8}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"ISam.5.8","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"1SA","sourceChapterCoverage":[5],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"42ccc1558899ce23d868f79ddc1f8f1d974f5a631bf59b00cd6fa32346352982","sourceItemName":"ISam.5.8","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":3279,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"1SA","canonicalOSIS":"1Sam","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"1Sam"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"1Sam.5.8","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"ISam.5.8","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=1Sam.5.9","r":"1Sam.5.9","rawR":"1Sam.5.9","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Sam.5.9","value":"1Sam.5.9"}],"t":"ref","v":"5:9"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," God often used ",{"c":["great panic"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," to incapacitate and overwhelm Israel’s enemies (",{"href":"?bref=1Sam.14.15","r":"1Sam.14.15","rawR":"1Sam.14.15","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Sam.14.15","value":"1Sam.14.15"}],"t":"ref","v":"14:15"},", ",{"href":"?bref=1Sam.14.20","r":"1Sam.14.20","rawR":"1Sam.14.20","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Sam.14.20","value":"1Sam.14.20"}],"t":"ref","v":"20"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Deut.7.23","r":"Deut.7.23","rawR":"Deut.7.23","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Deut.7.23","value":"Deut.7.23"}],"t":"ref","v":"Deut 7:23"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Ezek.22.5","r":"Ezek.22.5","rawR":"Ezek.22.5","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Ezek.22.5","value":"Ezek.22.5"}],"t":"ref","v":"Ezek 22:5"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Amos.3.9","r":"Amos.3.9","rawR":"Amos.3.9","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Amos.3.9","value":"Amos.3.9"}],"t":"ref","v":"Amos 3:9"},")."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:ISam.5.9","refs":[{"book":"1SA","endChapter":5,"endVerse":9,"startChapter":5,"startVerse":9}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"ISam.5.9","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"1SA","sourceChapterCoverage":[5],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"12bbecc7aec9b0a29ba33289f59eee15617ab5dd5aa887f8a0c32300241701df","sourceItemName":"ISam.5.9","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":3280,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"1SA","canonicalOSIS":"1Sam","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"1Sam"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"1Sam.5.9","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"ISam.5.9","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=1Sam.5.10","r":"1Sam.5.10","rawR":"1Sam.5.10","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Sam.5.10","value":"1Sam.5.10"}],"t":"ref","v":"5:10"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["Ekron"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"},{"c":[","],"sourceClass":"ital","t":"span"}," another of the five major Philistine cities (see ",{"href":"?bref=1Sam.6.17","r":"1Sam.6.17","rawR":"1Sam.6.17","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Sam.6.17","value":"1Sam.6.17"}],"t":"ref","v":"6:17"},"), was just five miles north of Gath."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:ISam.5.10","refs":[{"book":"1SA","endChapter":5,"endVerse":10,"startChapter":5,"startVerse":10}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"ISam.5.10","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNo